window.PhysicaRankPyqNeet_chemistry_equilibrium=[
{"id":"neet-2026-q048","pn":48,"y":"NEET 2026 · 3 May","tp":"Equilibrium","st":"Solubility product and pH","q":"In a qualitative analysis, Bi³⁺ is detected by appearance of precipitate of BiO(OH)(s). Calculate pH when the following equilibrium exists at 298 K.\nBiO(OH)(s) ⇌ BiO⁺(aq) + OH⁻(aq), K = 4×10⁻¹⁰\n(Given: log2 = 0.3010)","o":["4.699","8.714","9.301","5.286"],"c":2,"s":"K = [BiO⁺][OH⁻]/[BiO(OH)(s)]. The solid has activity 1, so K = [BiO⁺][OH⁻].\nLet each ion have concentration s. Then K = s².\ns = √K = √(4×10⁻¹⁰) = 2×10⁻⁵ M, so [OH⁻] = 2×10⁻⁵ M.\n[H⁺] = Kw/[OH⁻] = 10⁻¹⁴/(2×10⁻⁵) = (1/2)×10⁻⁹.\npH = –log[H⁺] = 9 + log2 = 9.301."},
{"id":"neet-2026-q060","pn":60,"y":"NEET 2026 · 3 May","tp":"Equilibrium","st":"Salt analysis (acetate test)","q":"In a test tube containing a salt, a few drops of dilute H₂SO₄ was added, which gave colourless vapours having the smell of vinegar. The vapours turned the blue litmus paper red. Identify the correct anion from the following :","o":["Acetate, CH₃COO⁻","Carbonate, CO₃²⁻","Sulphate, SO₄²⁻","Sulphate, S²⁻"],"c":0,"s":"Salt + dil. H₂SO₄ gives CH₃COOH vapour, which smells of vinegar.\nThe vapour is acidic, so it turns blue litmus paper red.\nSo the anion is acetate, CH₃COO⁻."},
{"id":"neet-2026-q068","pn":68,"y":"NEET 2026 · 3 May","tp":"Equilibrium","st":"Acid-base indicators","q":"Phenolphthalein is used as an indicator for the titration of sodium hydroxide solution against a standard solution of oxalic acid. The colour change that is observed at an alkaline pH close to the equivalence point during this titration is:","o":["pinkish red to yellow","yellow to pinkish red","colourless to pink","pink to colourless"],"c":2,"s":"Phenolphthalein is colourless before the end point.\nClose to the equivalence point (alkaline pH) it is pink.\nSo the colour change is colourless to pink."},
{"id":"neet-2026-q078","pn":78,"y":"NEET 2026 · 3 May","tp":"Equilibrium","st":"Buffer pH, Henderson equation","q":"At 298 K, a certain buffer solution contains equal concentrations of X⁻ and HX, Kb for X⁻ is 10⁻¹⁰. What is the pH of this buffer solution?","o":["2","10","4","6"],"c":2,"s":"HX ⇌ H⁺ + X⁻\nKa × Kb = Kw\nKa = Kw/Kb = 10⁻¹⁴/10⁻¹⁰ = 10⁻⁴\npKa = 4\npH = pKa + log([X⁻]/[HX])\nGiven [X⁻] = [HX], so log(1) = 0.\npH = 4 + 0 = 4"},
{"id":"neet-2026r-q086","pn":86,"y":"Re-NEET 2026 · 21 Jun","tp":"Equilibrium","st":"Solubility product and solubility","q":"The correct order of solubility of the given salts in water at 298 K is\nSalt — Ksp at 298 K\nAgBr — 5.0 × 10⁻¹³\nZn(OH)₂ — 1.0 × 10⁻¹⁵\nHg₂Cl₂ — 1.3 × 10⁻¹⁸","o":["AgBr > Zn(OH)₂ > Hg₂Cl₂","Hg₂Cl₂ > AgBr > Zn(OH)₂","Zn(OH)₂ > AgBr > Hg₂Cl₂","Hg₂Cl₂ > Zn(OH)₂ > AgBr"],"c":2,"s":"AgBr(s) ⇌ Ag⁺(aq) + Br⁻(aq).\nKsp = 5 × 10⁻¹³ = S², so S = √(5 × 10⁻¹³) = 7.07 × 10⁻⁷ mol/L.\nZn(OH)₂(s) ⇌ Zn²⁺(aq) + 2OH⁻(aq).\nKsp = 10⁻¹⁵ = (S)(2S)² = 4S³, so S = 0.63 × 10⁻⁵ = 6.3 × 10⁻⁶ mol/L.\nHg₂Cl₂(s) ⇌ Hg₂²⁺(aq) + 2Cl⁻(aq).\nKsp = 1.3 × 10⁻¹⁸ = 4S³, so S = 0.69 × 10⁻⁶ = 6.9 × 10⁻⁷ mol/L.\nCorrect order of solubility: Zn(OH)₂ > AgBr > Hg₂Cl₂."},
{"id":"neet-2026-q087","pn":87,"y":"NEET 2026 · 3 May","tp":"Equilibrium","st":"Kp and Kc relation","q":"Given below are certain reactions. Identify the reaction for which KP ≠ KC.","o":["H₂(g) + I₂(g) ⇌ 2HI(g)","N₂(g) + O₂(g) ⇌ 2NO(g)","N₂(g) + 3H₂(g) ⇌ 2NH₃(g)","H₂O(g) + CO(g) ⇌ H₂(g) + CO₂(g)"],"c":2,"s":"KP = KC(RT)^Δng.\nKP = KC when Δng = 0, and KP ≠ KC when Δng ≠ 0.\n(1) H₂ + I₂ ⇌ 2HI: Δng = 0.\n(2) N₂ + O₂ ⇌ 2NO: Δng = 0.\n(3) N₂ + 3H₂ ⇌ 2NH₃: Δng = –2, so KP ≠ KC.\n(4) H₂O + CO ⇌ H₂ + CO₂: Δng = 0."},
{"id":"neet-2025-q048","pn":48,"y":"NEET 2025","tp":"Equilibrium","st":"Cation analysis groups","q":"Match List I with List II\nList-I (Ion) — List-II (Group Number in Cation Analysis)\nA. Co²⁺ — I. Group-I\nB. Mg²⁺ — II. Group-III\nC. Pb²⁺ — III. Group-IV\nD. Al³⁺ — IV. Group-VI\nChoose the correct answer from the options given below :","o":["A-III, B-IV, C-II, D-I","A-III, B-IV, C-I, D-II","A-III, B-II, C-IV, D-I","A-III, B-II, C-I, D-IV"],"c":1,"s":"Co²⁺ is in Group-IV.\nMg²⁺ is in Group-VI.\nPb²⁺ is in Group-I.\nAl³⁺ is in Group-III.\nSo A-III, B-IV, C-I, D-II."},
{"id":"neet-2025-q059","pn":59,"y":"NEET 2025","tp":"Equilibrium","st":"Ionisation of polyprotic acids","q":"Phosphoric acid ionizes in three steps with their ionization constant values Ka₁, Ka₂ and Ka₃, respectively, while K is the overall ionization constant. Which of the following statements are true?\nA. log K = log Ka₁ + log Ka₂ + log Ka₃\nB. H₃PO₄ is a stronger acid than H₂PO₄⁻ and HPO₄²⁻\nC. Ka₁ > Ka₂ > Ka₃\nD. Ka₁ = (Ka₃ + Ka₂)/2\nChoose the correct answer from the options given below :","o":["A and B only","A and C only","B, C and D only","A, B and C only"],"c":3,"s":"H₃PO₄ is a stronger acid than H₂PO₄⁻ and HPO₄²⁻.\nH₃PO₄(aq) ⇌ H⁺(aq) + H₂PO₄⁻(aq), Ka₁ = 7.5 × 10⁻³.\nH₂PO₄⁻(aq) ⇌ H⁺(aq) + HPO₄²⁻(aq), Ka₂ = 6.2 × 10⁻⁸.\nHPO₄²⁻(aq) ⇌ H⁺(aq) + PO₄³⁻(aq), Ka₃ = 1.7 × 10⁻¹².\nSo Ka₁ > Ka₂ > Ka₃.\nK = Ka₁ × Ka₂ × Ka₃, so log K = log Ka₁ + log Ka₂ + log Ka₃.\nAnswer: A, B and C only."},
{"id":"neet-2025-q073","pn":73,"y":"NEET 2025","tp":"Equilibrium","st":"Kp and Kc relation","q":"For the reaction A(g) ⇌ 2B(g), the backward reaction rate constant is higher than the forward reaction rate constant by a factor of 2500, at 1000 K.\n[Given : R = 0.0831 L atm mol⁻¹ K⁻¹]\nKP for the reaction at 1000 K is","o":["83.1","2.077 × 10⁵","0.033","0.021"],"c":2,"s":"Kc = k_f/k_b = 1/2500\nKp = Kc(RT)^Δn_g, where Δn_g = 2 – 1 = 1\n= (1/2500) × 0.0831 × 1000\n= 0.033"},
{"id":"neet-2025-q088","pn":88,"y":"NEET 2025","tp":"Equilibrium","st":"Le Chatelier's principle","q":"Higher yield of NO in N₂(g) + O₂(g) ⇌ 2NO(g) can be obtained at\n[ΔH of the reaction = +180.7 kJ mol⁻¹]\nA. Higher temperature\nB. Lower temperature\nC. Higher concentration of N₂\nD. Higher concentration of O₂\nChoose the correct answer from the options given below :","o":["A, D only","B, C only","B, C, D only","A, C, D only"],"c":3,"s":"Yield of the product generally depends on temperature, concentration of reactant(s) and product(s), and pressure.\nThis is an endothermic reaction (ΔH = +180.7 kJ mol⁻¹), so an increase in temperature shifts the equilibrium in the forward direction and increases the yield of NO.\nIncrease in concentration of reactants (N₂ and O₂) also shifts the equilibrium in the forward direction and increases the yield of NO.\nHence, (A), (C) and (D) only will increase the yield of NO."},
{"id":"neet-2024-q072","pn":72,"y":"NEET 2024 · 5 May","tp":"Equilibrium","st":"Reaction quotient","q":"For the reaction 2A ⇌ B + C, K_C = 4 × 10⁻³. At a given time, the composition of reaction mixture is : [A] = [B] = [C] = 2 × 10⁻³ M.\nThen which of the following is correct?","o":["Reaction has a tendency to go in backward direction","Reaction has gone to completion in forward direction","Reaction is at equilibrium.","Reaction has a tendency to go in forward direction"],"c":0,"s":"Find the reaction quotient Q_C and compare it with K_C.\nQ_C = [B][C]/[A]² = (2 × 10⁻³)(2 × 10⁻³)/(2 × 10⁻³)² = 1.\nQ_C = 1 is greater than K_C = 4 × 10⁻³.\nSo the reaction tends to move in the backward direction."},
{"id":"neet-2024-q074","pn":74,"y":"NEET 2024 · 5 May","tp":"Equilibrium","st":"Relation between Kp and Kc","q":"In which of the following equilibria, Kp and Kc are NOT equal ?","o":["CO(g) + H₂O(g) ⇌ CO₂(g) + H₂(g)","2BrCl(g) ⇌ Br₂(g) + Cl₂(g)","PCl₅(g) ⇌ PCl₃(g) + Cl₂(g)","H₂(g) + I₂(g) ⇌ 2HI(g)"],"c":2,"s":"K_p = K_c (RT)^(Δn_g).\nK_p differs from K_c only when Δn_g ≠ 0, where Δn_g = moles of gaseous products – moles of gaseous reactants.\n(1) Δn_g = 2 – 2 = 0.\n(2) Δn_g = 2 – 2 = 0.\n(3) Δn_g = 2 – 1 = 1.\n(4) Δn_g = 2 – 2 = 0.\nSo only option (3) has K_p ≠ K_c."},
{"id":"neet-2024-q093","pn":93,"y":"NEET 2024 · 5 May","tp":"Equilibrium","st":"Qualitative analysis cation groups","q":"Given below are certain cations. Using inorganic qualitative analysis, arrange them in increasing group number from 0 to VI.\nA. Al³⁺\nB. Cu²⁺\nC. Ba²⁺\nD. Co²⁺\nE. Mg²⁺\nChoose the correct answer from the options given below :","o":["E, C, D, B, A","E, A, B, C, D","B, A, D, C, E","B, C, A, D, E"],"c":2,"s":"Groups of the cations: Cu²⁺ is Group II, Al³⁺ is Group III, Co²⁺ is Group IV, Ba²⁺ is Group V, Mg²⁺ is Group VI.\nIncreasing group number: Cu²⁺ (B) < Al³⁺ (A) < Co²⁺ (D) < Ba²⁺ (C) < Mg²⁺ (E).\nThe order is B, A, D, C, E.","sfig":{"w":200,"h":152,"alt":"Table of groups and cations: Group II Cu2+, Group III Al3+, Group IV Co2+, Group V Ba2+, Group VI Mg2+.","d":[["r",10,10,180,134],["l",100,10,100,144],["l",10,34,190,34],["l",10,56,190,56],["l",10,78,190,78],["l",10,100,190,100],["l",10,122,190,122],["t",55,26,"Group","m"],["t",145,26,"Cations","m"],["t",55,49,"Group-II","m"],["t",145,49,"Cu²⁺","m"],["t",55,71,"Group-III","m"],["t",145,71,"Al³⁺","m"],["t",55,93,"Group-IV","m"],["t",145,93,"Co²⁺","m"],["t",55,115,"Group-V","m"],["t",145,115,"Ba²⁺","m"],["t",55,137,"Group-VI","m"],["t",145,137,"Mg²⁺","m"]]}},
{"id":"neet-2024-q096","pn":96,"y":"NEET 2024 · 5 May","tp":"Equilibrium","st":"Degree of dissociation from Kc","q":"Consider the following reaction in a sealed vessel at equilibrium with concentrations of N₂ = 3.0 × 10⁻³ M, O₂ = 4.2 × 10⁻³ M and NO = 2.8 × 10⁻³ M.\n2NO(g) ⇌ N₂(g) + O₂(g)\nIf 0.1 mol L⁻¹ of NO(g) is taken in closed vessel, what will be degree of dissociation (α) of NO(g) at equilibrium ?","o":["0.8889","0.717","0.00889","0.0889"],"c":1,"s":"Kc = [N₂][O₂]/[NO]².\nKc = (3 × 10⁻³ × 4.2 × 10⁻³)/(2.8 × 10⁻³ × 2.8 × 10⁻³) = 1.607.\nStart: NO = 0.1, N₂ = 0, O₂ = 0.\nAt equilibrium: NO = 0.1 – 0.1α, N₂ = 0.05α, O₂ = 0.05α.\nKc = (0.05α × 0.05α)/(0.1 – 0.1α)² = (0.05)²α²/(0.01(1 – α)²).\nSo α²/(1 – α)² = 1.607 × (0.1)²/(0.05)².\nTaking the square root: α/(1 – α) = 1.27 × 0.1/0.05 = 2.54.\nα = 2.54 – 2.54α.\n3.54α = 2.54.\nα = 2.54/3.54 = 0.717."},
{"id":"neet-2023-q051","pn":51,"y":"NEET 2023 · 7 May","tp":"Equilibrium","st":"Lewis acids and bases","q":"Amongst the given options which of the following molecules/ion acts as a Lewis acid ?","o":["OH⁻","NH₃","H₂O","BF₃"],"c":3,"s":"A Lewis acid accepts a lone pair of electrons.\nIt needs a vacant orbital in its outer shell to do so.\nBF₃ has a vacant orbital on boron, so it is a Lewis acid.\nH₂O, OH⁻ and NH₃ have lone pairs to donate, so they are Lewis bases."},
{"id":"neet-2023-q088","pn":88,"y":"NEET 2023 · 7 May","tp":"Equilibrium","st":"Gibbs energy and equilibrium constant","q":"The equilibrium concentrations of the species in the reaction A + B ⇌ C + D are 2, 3, 10 and 6 mol L⁻¹, respectively at 300 K. ΔG° for the reaction is (R = 2 cal/mol K)","o":["–13.73 cal","–1372.60 cal","–137.26 cal","–1381.80 cal"],"c":3,"s":"K = [C][D]/([A][B]) = (10 × 6)/(2 × 3) = 10.\nΔG° = –RT ln K = –2.303 RT log K.\n= –2.303 × 2 × 300 × log 10.\n= –1381.8 cal."},
{"id":"neet-2022r-q067","pn":67,"y":"Re-NEET 2022 · 4 Sep","tp":"Equilibrium","st":"pH of weak acid","q":"0.01 M acetic acid solution is 1% ionised, then pH of this acetic acid solution is :","o":["1","3","2","4"],"c":3,"s":"CH₃COOH ⇌ CH₃COO⁻ + H⁺.\nAt equilibrium [H⁺] = Cα.\n[H⁺] = 0.01 × (1/100) = 10⁻⁴ M.\npH = –log[H⁺] = 4."},
{"id":"neet-2022-q083","pn":83,"y":"NEET 2022 · 17 Jul","tp":"Equilibrium","st":"Buffer solutions","q":"The pH of the solution containing 50 mL each of 0.10 M sodium acetate and 0.01 M acetic acid is [Given pKa of CH₃COOH = 4.57]","o":["3.57","4.57","2.57","5.57"],"c":3,"s":"The mixture is a weak acid with the salt of its conjugate base, so it is an acidic buffer.\npH = pKa + log([salt]/[acid]).\nEqual volumes are mixed, so the ratio stays 0.10/0.01 = 10.\npH = 4.57 + log 10 = 4.57 + 1 = 5.57."},
{"id":"neet-2022r-q090","pn":90,"y":"Re-NEET 2022 · 4 Sep","tp":"Equilibrium","st":"Relation between Kp and Kc","q":"Kp for the following reaction is 3.0 at 1000 K.\nCO₂(g) + C(s) ⇌ 2CO(g)\nWhat will be the value of Kc for the reaction at the same temperature ?\n(Given – R = 0.083 L bar K⁻¹ mol⁻¹)","o":["3.6","0.36","3.6 × 10⁻²","3.6 × 10⁻³"],"c":2,"s":"Kp = Kc(RT)^Δng.\nΔng = 2 - 1 = 1 (only gases count).\n3 = Kc × (0.083 × 1000).\nKc = 3/(0.083 × 1000) = 3.6 × 10⁻²."},
{"id":"neet-2022-q092","pn":92,"y":"NEET 2022 · 17 Jul","tp":"Equilibrium","st":"Equilibrium constant Kc","q":"3O₂(g) ⇌ 2O₃(g)\nfor the above reaction at 298 K, Kc is found to be 3.0 × 10⁻⁵⁹. If the concentration of O₂ at equilibrium is 0.040 M then concentration of O₃ in M is","o":["1.9 × 10⁻⁶³","2.4 × 10³¹","1.2 × 10²¹","4.38 × 10⁻³²"],"c":3,"s":"Kc = [O₃]²/[O₂]³.\n[O₃]² = Kc × [O₂]³ = 3 × 10⁻⁵⁹ × (0.04)³ = 1.9 × 10⁻⁶³ = 19 × 10⁻⁶⁴.\n[O₃] = √(19 × 10⁻⁶⁴) = 4.38 × 10⁻³² M."},
{"id":"neet-2021-q068","pn":68,"y":"NEET 2021 · 12 Sep","tp":"Equilibrium","st":"pH of salt of weak acid and weak base","q":"The pKb of dimethylamine and pKa of acetic acid are 3.27 and 4.77 respectively at T (K). The correct option for the pH of dimethylammonium acetate solution is :","o":["6.25","8.50","5.50","7.75"],"c":3,"s":"The salt comes from a weak acid and a weak base, so pH = 7 + (1/2)(pKa – pKb).\n= 7 + (1/2)(4.77 – 3.27) = 7 + 0.75.\npH = 7.75."},
{"id":"neet-2020-q168","pn":168,"y":"NEET 2020 · 13 Sep","tp":"Equilibrium","st":"Solubility product","q":"Find out the solubility of Ni(OH)₂ in 0.1 M NaOH. Given that the ionic product of Ni(OH)₂ is 2 × 10⁻¹⁵","o":["2 × 10⁻⁸ M","1 × 10⁻¹³ M","1 × 10⁸ M","2 × 10⁻¹³ M"],"c":3,"s":"NaOH gives 0.1 M OH⁻.\nLet the solubility of Ni(OH)₂ be S: [Ni²⁺] = S, [OH⁻] = 0.1 + 2S ≈ 0.1.\nK = S(0.1)² = 2 × 10⁻¹⁵.\nS = 2 × 10⁻¹⁵/10⁻² = 2 × 10⁻¹³ M."},
{"id":"neet-2020-q178","pn":178,"y":"NEET 2020 · 13 Sep","tp":"Equilibrium","st":"Common ion effect","q":"HCl was passed through solution of CaCl₂, MgCl₂ and NaCl. Which of the following compound(s) crystallise(s) ?","o":["Only NaCl","Only MgCl₂","NaCl, MgCl₂ and CaCl₂","Both MgCl₂ and CaCl₂"],"c":0,"s":"HCl adds Cl⁻ ions, raising [Cl⁻].\nThe ionic product [Na⁺][Cl⁻] first exceeds the solubility product of NaCl, so NaCl separates (common ion effect).\nMgCl₂ and CaCl₂ are much more soluble and stay in solution."},
{"id":"neet-2019-q154","pn":154,"y":"NEET 2019 · 5 May","tp":"Equilibrium","st":"Solubility product","q":"pH of a saturated solution of Ca(OH)₂ is 9. The solubility product (Ksp) of Ca(OH)₂ is -","o":["0.125 × 10⁻¹⁵","0.5 × 10⁻¹⁰","0.5 × 10⁻¹⁵","0.25 × 10⁻¹⁰"],"c":2,"s":"pH = 9, so pOH = 5 and [OH⁻] = 10⁻⁵ M.\nCa(OH)₂ ⇌ Ca²⁺ + 2OH⁻; if s is the solubility, [OH⁻] = 2s, so s = 0.5 × 10⁻⁵ M.\nKsp = s × (2s)² = (0.5 × 10⁻⁵)(10⁻⁵)² = 0.5 × 10⁻¹⁵."},
{"id":"neet-2019-q174","pn":174,"y":"NEET 2019 · 5 May","tp":"Equilibrium","st":"Brønsted acids and bases","q":"Conjugate base for Bronsted acids H₂O and HF are :","o":["OH⁻ and F⁻, respectively","H₃O⁺ and H₂F⁺, respectively","OH⁻ and H₂F⁺, respectively","H₃O⁺ and F⁻, respectively"],"c":0,"s":"A conjugate base is formed when the acid loses H⁺.\nH₂O − H⁺ = OH⁻.\nHF − H⁺ = F⁻."},
{"id":"neet-2019-q175","pn":175,"y":"NEET 2019 · 5 May","tp":"Equilibrium","st":"Buffer solutions","q":"Which will make basic buffer?","o":["100 mL of 0.1 M HCl + 200 mL of 0.1 M NH₄OH","100 mL of 0.1 M HCl + 100 mL of 0.1 M NH₄OH","50 mL of 0.1 M NaOH + 25 mL of 0.1 M CH₃COOH","100 mL of 0.1 M CH₃COOH + 100 mL of 0.1 M NaOH"],"c":0,"s":"A basic buffer is a weak base plus its salt, so the weak base must be left over after the strong acid is added.\nOption 1: NH₄OH = 200 × 0.1 = 20 mmol, HCl = 100 × 0.1 = 10 mmol. After reaction 10 mmol NH₄OH is left with 10 mmol NH₄Cl: a basic buffer.\nOption 2: 10 mmol each, so only salt (NH₄Cl) is left: no buffer.\nOption 3: NaOH = 5 mmol, CH₃COOH = 2.5 mmol; the acid is used up and 2.5 mmol strong base is left over: not a buffer.\nOption 4: 10 mmol each, only CH₃COONa is left: not a buffer.\nOnly option 1 is a basic buffer.\nNote: Option 2 is printed 'NHOH' in the source; read as NH₄OH."},
{"id":"neet-2018-q069","pn":69,"y":"NEET 2018 · 6 May","tp":"Equilibrium","st":"pH of mixed acid and base","q":"Following solutions were prepared by mixing different volumes of NaOH and HCl of different concentrations : (a) 60 mL (M)/(10) HCl + 40 mL (M)/(10) NaOH (b) 55 mL (M)/(10) HCl + 45 mL (M)/(10) NaOH (c) 75 mL (M)/(5) HCl + 25 mL (M)/(5) NaOH (d) 100 mL (M)/(10) HCl + 100 mL (M)/(10) NaOH. pH of which one of them will be equal to 1 ?","o":["b","a","d","c"],"c":3,"s":"Find the excess H⁺ after mixing and divide by total volume.\n(a) 6 – 4 = 2 meq in 100 mL: 0.02 M, pH = 1.7.\n(b) 5.5 – 4.5 = 1 meq in 100 mL: 0.01 M, pH = 2.\n(c) 15 – 5 = 10 meq in 100 mL: 0.1 M, pH = 1.\n(d) 10 – 10 = 0: neutral, pH = 7.\nSo (c)."},
{"id":"neet-2018-q070","pn":70,"y":"NEET 2018 · 6 May","tp":"Equilibrium","st":"Solubility product","q":"The solubility of BaSO₄ in water is 2.42 × 10⁻³ gL⁻¹ at 298 K. The value of its solubility product (Ksp) will be (Given molar mass of BaSO₄ = 233 g mol⁻¹)","o":["1.08 × 10⁻¹⁰ mol² L⁻²","1.08 × 10⁻¹² mol² L⁻²","1.08 × 10⁻¹⁴ mol² L⁻²","1.08 × 10⁻⁸ mol² L⁻²"],"c":0,"s":"Convert to mol/L: S = (2.42 × 10⁻³)/(233) = 1.04 × 10⁻⁵ mol/L.\nBaSO₄ ⇌ Ba²⁺ + SO₄²⁻, so Ksp = S × S = S².\nKsp = (1.04 × 10⁻⁵)² = 1.08 × 10⁻¹⁰ mol² L⁻²."},
{"id":"neet-2018-q077","pn":77,"y":"NEET 2018 · 6 May","tp":"Equilibrium","st":"Le Chatelier's principle","q":"Which one of the following conditions will favour maximum formation of the product in the reaction A₂(g) + B₂(g) ⇌ X₂(g) ΔrH = – X kJ ?","o":["Low temperature and high pressure","Low temperature and low pressure","High temperature and high pressure","High temperature and low pressure"],"c":0,"s":"The reaction is exothermic (ΔH negative), so low temperature favours the forward reaction.\nMoles of gas fall from 2 to 1 (Δng < 0), so high pressure favours the forward reaction.\nBest condition: low temperature and high pressure."},
{"id":"neet-2017-q009","pn":9,"y":"NEET 2017 · 7 May","tp":"Equilibrium","st":"Solubility product","q":"Concentration of the Ag⁺ ions in a saturated solution of Ag2C2O4 is 2.2 × 10⁻⁴ mol L⁻¹. Solubility product of Ag2C2O4 is :","o":["5.3 × 10⁻¹²","2.42 × 10⁻⁸","2.66 × 10⁻¹²","4.5 × 10⁻¹¹"],"c":0,"s":"Ag2C2O4 ⇌ 2Ag⁺ + C2O4²⁻. If solubility is s, [Ag⁺] = 2s and [C2O4²⁻] = s.\n2s = 2.2 × 10⁻⁴, so s = 1.1 × 10⁻⁴ mol/L.\nKsp = (2s)² × s = 4s³.\nKsp = 4 × (1.1 × 10⁻⁴)³ = 4 × 1.331 × 10⁻¹² = 5.3 × 10⁻¹²."},
{"id":"neet-2017-q015","pn":15,"y":"NEET 2017 · 7 May","tp":"Equilibrium","st":"Combining equilibrium constants","q":"The equilibrium constants of the following are :\nN2 + 3H2 ⇌ 2NH3 (K1)\nN2 + O2 ⇌ 2NO (K2)\nH2 + ½O2 → H2O (K3)\nThe equilibrium constant (K) of the reaction : 2NH3 + (5)/(2) O2 ⇌ 2NO + 3H2O, will be :","o":["K2² K3/K1","K1 K3³/K2","K2 K3³/K1","K2 K3/K1"],"c":2,"s":"Target: 2NH3 + 5/2 O2 gives 2NO + 3H2O.\nReverse equation 1 (K becomes 1/K1): 2NH3 gives N2 + 3H2.\nAdd equation 2 (K2): N2 + O2 gives 2NO.\nAdd 3 × equation 3 (K becomes K3³): 3H2 + 3/2 O2 gives 3H2O.\nAdding: 2NH3 + 5/2 O2 gives 2NO + 3H2O.\nWhen reactions are added, K values multiply: K = K2 × K3³ / K1."},
{"id":"neet-2017-q020","pn":20,"y":"NEET 2017 · 7 May","tp":"Equilibrium","st":"Kp and Boyle's law","q":"A 20 litre container at 400 K contains CO2(g) at pressure 0.4 atm and an excess of SrO (neglect the volume of solid SrO). The volume of the container is now decreased by moving the movable piston fitted in the container. The maximum volume of the container, when pressure of CO2 attains its maximum value, will be :\n(Given that : SrCO3(s) ⇌ SrO(s) + CO2(g), Kp = 1.6 atm)","o":["2 litre","5 litre","10 litre","4 litre"],"c":1,"s":"For SrCO3(s) ⇌ SrO(s) + CO2(g), Kp = P(CO2).\nSo the highest pressure CO2 can have is 1.6 atm; beyond that SrCO3 forms.\nUp to this point only the gas is compressed at constant T, so P1V1 = P2V2.\n0.4 × 20 = 1.6 × V2, so V2 = 5 L."},
{"id":"neet-2017-q039","pn":39,"y":"NEET 2017 · 7 May","tp":"Equilibrium","st":"Catalyst and equilibrium","q":"Which one of the following statements is not correct ?","o":["Coenzymes increase the catalytic activity of enzyme","Catalyst does not initiate and reaction","The value of equilibrium constant is changed in the presence of a catalyst in the reaction at equilibrium .","Enzymes catalyse mainly bio-chemical reaction."],"c":2,"s":"A catalyst speeds up the forward and backward reactions equally.\nSo it only helps the system reach equilibrium sooner; it does not change K.\nThe statement that K changes with a catalyst is wrong."}
];
